                "entry_id": "ee92cd22-8af4-3808-96db-df51b0d8a88d",
                "word": "acroamatic",
                "pos": "adj",
                "lang_code": "en",
                "forms": [
                    {
                        "form_id": "ad117429-3a63-4fea-a375-384d8192781e",
                        "entry_id": "ee92cd22-8af4-3808-96db-df51b0d8a88d",
                        "tags": [
                            "comparative"
                        ],
                        "form": "more acroamatic"
                    },
                    {
                        "form_id": "9296e321-b3f3-4683-a71f-415d61993d68",
                        "entry_id": "ee92cd22-8af4-3808-96db-df51b0d8a88d",
                        "tags": [
                            "superlative"
                        ],
                        "form": "most acroamatic"
                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "f2e613f0-a8fe-37be-b2e3-67546391b9ad",
                        "entry_id": "ee92cd22-8af4-3808-96db-df51b0d8a88d",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Of or pertaining to hearing."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "archaic",
                            "rare"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "8ee65b9d-bcdc-4e4e-aded-083ee5aa9936",
                                "sense_id": "f2e613f0-a8fe-37be-b2e3-67546391b9ad",
                                "text": "Prate they not cataracts of insensible noise, / That with obstreperous cadence cracks the organs / Acroamatick, till the deaf auditor / Admires the words he heares not?"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "85fedcf0-b9b8-4205-9af2-280227a81bb0",
                                "sense_id": "f2e613f0-a8fe-37be-b2e3-67546391b9ad",
                                "text": "I should prefer, therefore, to call the former acroamatic, or audible (discursive) proofs, because they can be carried out by words only (the object in thought), rather than demonstrations, which, as the very term implies, depend on the intuition of the object."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "f6c6b7d4-98ec-437d-abf6-3155a2a71074",
                                "sense_id": "f2e613f0-a8fe-37be-b2e3-67546391b9ad",
                                "text": "The idea that the first thought, as an internal event, is always also dialogical, that is, possesses a communicative quality in itself, is connected to its acroamatic origin."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "6006b642-7cb7-3926-9540-09e00ba55569",
                        "entry_id": "ee92cd22-8af4-3808-96db-df51b0d8a88d",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Esoteric, abstruse; (in particular) taught orally to select students and not disseminated."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "968e3610-1693-407e-96c0-c62e9bf1a916",
                                "sense_id": "6006b642-7cb7-3926-9540-09e00ba55569",
                                "text": "How to begin such warfare against sovereign religion in times ruled by the very zealots against whom one must fight? How else but covertly, enigmatically, in the acroamatic manner practiced so beautifully in Holy War?"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "e79f8337-58ce-45e0-bb6b-b1112b3b35c1",
                                "sense_id": "6006b642-7cb7-3926-9540-09e00ba55569",
                                "text": "Repeatedly in the Gospels Jesus takes his disciples aside to offer them acroamatic teachings not intended for the mass of listeners or to explain a secret meaning to some parable with which he has entertained the larger crowd."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "13cf813a-bd19-311a-b85d-b5fa17c9f4ec",
                        "entry_id": "ee92cd22-8af4-3808-96db-df51b0d8a88d",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Based on lectures or exposition by monologue."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "f865f100-f553-401c-b221-c34217e3883a",
                                "sense_id": "13cf813a-bd19-311a-b85d-b5fa17c9f4ec",
                                "text": "Coordinate term: erotematic"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "55b45452-d50d-4ad3-a1d0-785bd598e9e7",
                                "sense_id": "13cf813a-bd19-311a-b85d-b5fa17c9f4ec",
                                "text": "Up to that time the “acroamatic” method had been used in which the catechist gave a lecture, and the children were only listeners (ἀκροᾶσθαι)."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "99bcd469-adb7-403a-a392-fc4b1e6e6d28",
                                "sense_id": "13cf813a-bd19-311a-b85d-b5fa17c9f4ec",
                                "text": "[Questions’] employment here does not mark a shift from the acroamatic (lecture-based) to the erotematic (interrogatory) method, for the answers are not known."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
